            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your 1987 house is in good shape and is very close to being perfectly in balance (from an air leakage perspective) for the type of mechanical equipment currently being used. If you proceeded with a DHERP retrofit of your house and also upgraded your heating &amp; hot water equipment, there is some air leakage reduction you would enjoy:



** Air leakage associated with some of the windows &amp; patio doors (not significant)



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** Around the dryer vent as it passes through the wall



** The intentional holes (co-vented chimney for furnace &amp; hot water tank, plus the 6" combustion air supply pipe) would be eliminated if the new furnace and on-demand hot water were high-efficiency, condensing, direct vent units.



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 98 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.5 GJ, and qualify for  500 in rebates. For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x6 (R20) and I have modeled the addition of another R10 (eg. 2" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 198 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add 3" (R15), the rebate increases to $5,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

Your basement has 4' of lower concrete walls (insulated inside) and 4' of upper pony walls (same construction as main floor upper walls (2x6@16 R20). If you add exterior wall insulation to the upper floor, you will no doubt add the same amount of exterior wall insulation to the pony walls. I modeled adding the same R10 to the exterior of these basement pony walls.



Savings: By adding R10 of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior of your basement pony walls, you could potentially save $ 128 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.8 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 625 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows>

You mentioned that if you were to proceed with an exterior siding upgrade for your house, this would be an ideal time to add more exterior wall insulation and let our new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program help pay for the cost of the new insulation. In conjunction with this, you indicated that you may even consider upgrading the older original windows ............ and this is something I can model for you.



I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of the original windows (includes the 2 x glass patio doors) with new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing the existing windows (and 2 patio doors) with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 444 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 20.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates (12 windows max @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <doors>

DOORS



Both of the exterior doors in this house are steel insulated doors with double pane glass windows. The doors are generally in good shape, but I recommend you replace the weather stripping around the interior of the frame to reduce draft, and increase comfort. This is especially important in the master bedroom.



Rebates: There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre oil-fired hot water tank (a rated 0.60 Energy Factor), co-vented up a chimney with the furnace. I mentioned that I would provide the estimated annual savings of replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a Uniform Energy Factor of 0.91 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current oil-fired hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing propane-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.91 UEF, could potentially save $ 6 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

Consistent with what I said for the furnace upgrade ....... I realize the estimated fuel savings above of only $6 sounds low. This is because fuel prices tend to have alot of fluctuation and fuel oil prices were quite low (compared to propane) when we completed our most recent Fuel Cost Library in December or 2020 (fuel rates for Hay River are summarized later in this report). If I used our previous 2019 fuel cost rates, the annual savings above would have been $ 133. The Arctic Energy Alliance is not promoting fuels based on their costs - we try to consider a balance between fuel costs, efficiency of the heating equipment available and the overall GHG reductions that can be achieved.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a relatively new, higher efficiency oil l furnace with a rated efficiency of 86.5%.  For your curiosity, we talked about what the estimated energy &amp; cost savings would be if you had installed an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 26 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 17.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Yes ....... I realize the estimated fuel savings of only $26 sounds low. This is because fuel prices tend to have alot of fluctuation and fuel oil prices were quite low (compared to propane) when we completed our most recent Fuel Cost Library in December or 2020 (fuel rates for Hay River are summarized later in this report). If I used our previous 2019 fuel cost rates, the annual savings above would have been $ 872. The Arctic Energy Alliance is not promoting fuels based on their costs - we try to consider a balance between fuel costs, efficiency of the heating equipment available and the overall GHG reductions that can be achieved.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



